# Root .dockerignore -- resolves `docs/problems/problems_final_review.md` P2-12.
#
# `services/control-plane/Dockerfile` and
# `services/governance-service/Dockerfile` both build with the
# repository root as their context (see each Dockerfile's own header
# comment) and only `COPY` explicit subpaths (`libs/contracts`,
# `services/control-plane`, etc.) -- never `COPY . .` -- so they were
# never at risk of accidentally baking an unwanted file into an image
# layer. But without a root `.dockerignore`, Docker still walks and
# sends this entire repository (including `.git/`, every service's
# `__pycache__`/`.venv`, `frontend/node_modules`, `data/tmp/` generator
# output, and the `.mypy_cache`/`.ruff_cache`/`.spark-runtime`
# directories this repo accumulates locally) to the Docker daemon as
# the build context before any `COPY` filtering happens. This trims
# that transfer to what these Dockerfiles could ever actually need.
.git/
.github/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.spark-runtime/
.pytest_cache/
**/__pycache__/
**/*.pyc
**/.venv/
**/venv/
frontend/node_modules/
frontend/dist/
frontend/test-results/
frontend/playwright-report/
data/tmp/
# Anchored to the repository root (a leading `/` in a .dockerignore
# pattern, same syntax as .gitignore) -- deliberately NOT a bare
# `docs/`/`*.md`, which would also match `libs/contracts/README.md`,
# `services/control-plane/README.md`, and
# `services/governance-service/README.md`: each is required at build
# time (`pyproject.toml`'s `readme = "README.md"`) for `pip wheel` to
# build that package's wheel inside the Docker build stage -- excluding
# them would break the build, not just trim it.
/docs/
/*.md
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
